  "position": 1,
  "status": "draft",
  "title": "Area of a parallelogram and trapezium",
  "summary": "Calculate areas and recover missing dimensions using perpendicular height.",
  "outcomes": [
    "5.1"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [],
  "estimated_minutes": 55,
  "objectives": [
    "Calculate the area of a parallelogram from its base and perpendicular height.",
    "Calculate the area of a trapezium from its parallel sides and perpendicular height.",
    "Rearrange area formulae to find an unknown dimension."
  ],
  "mastery_policy_key": "g5-default-v1",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-51-method",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Core method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Calculate areas and recover missing dimensions using perpendicular height. Keep every measurement in compatible units before substituting."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "A=bh,\\qquad A=\\frac12(a+b)h"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-01-visual",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Read the diagram",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The diagram shows the two shapes in the first worked example. In each shape the dashed line is the perpendicular height: it meets the base at 90°, and the small square marks the right angle. A parallelogram has area base × perpendicular height. A trapezium has area one half of the sum of its parallel sides, multiplied by the perpendicular height. Neither formula uses a sloping side."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g5-lesson-01-diagram"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-01-reasoning",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Reasoning method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Any side of a parallelogram can be the base; pair it with the perpendicular height to that side. For a trapezium, first identify the pair of parallel sides, then the perpendicular distance between them. Keep units consistent and square the final length unit, because area measures two-dimensional coverage."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-51-example",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: area of a parallelogram and a trapezium",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The diagram shows a parallelogram with base 10 cm and perpendicular height 6 cm, and a trapezium with parallel sides 7 cm and 13 cm and perpendicular height 5 cm. Find the area of each shape."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Parallelogram: multiply the base by the perpendicular height. The sloping side is not needed."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "A=bh=10\\times6=60\\text{ cm}^2"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Trapezium: add the parallel sides first, then halve and multiply by the perpendicular height."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "A=\\frac12(7+13)(5)=\\frac12(20)(5)=50\\text{ cm}^2"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The trapezium's area lies between 7 × 5 = 35 cm² and 13 × 5 = 65 cm², the areas of rectangles on its shorter and longer parallel sides. Both answers are in square centimetres."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-01-example-2",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: find a missing parallel side",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A trapezium has area 126 cm², perpendicular height 9 cm and one parallel side 10 cm. Find the other parallel side."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Substitute the known values into the trapezium formula, with b the unknown parallel side."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "126=\\frac12(10+b)\\times9"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Multiply both sides by 2, then divide both sides by 9."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "252=9(10+b)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad28=10+b"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "b=18\\text{ cm}"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Substitution gives ½ × (10 + 18) × 9 = 14 × 9 = 126 cm²."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-01-pitfalls",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Common errors and checks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Do not use a sloping side as the height. Do not forget to add the parallel sides before halving. State cm² or m² for an area and check that a recovered length is positive and plausible."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-51-recall",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A parallelogram has base 9 cm and perpendicular height 7 cm. Find its area in cm²."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "63",
        "canonical_latex": "63",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use base × perpendicular height: 9 × 7 = 63."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-01-summary",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Method checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Area of a parallelogram = base × perpendicular height. Area of a trapezium = ½ × (sum of the parallel sides) × perpendicular height."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use the height that meets the base at 90°, never a sloping side. To find a missing length, substitute what you know and undo the operations in reverse order. Give areas in square units."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
